WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.939 --> 00:00:07.200
Okay everybody — major, major Game of Thrones
spoilers here.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.929
For the last five seasons of Game of Thrones,
we’ve understood the show to be a linear

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:18.240
storyline. One thing leads to another thing,
to another thing — you get the idea.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.010
But in the past few episodes, that’s totally
changed. In his training with the Three Eyed

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.360
Raven, Bran Stark has learned how to travel
through time in dreamlike visions.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.570
And that makes Game of Thrones one of a whole
lot of works of fiction that have experimented

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.629
with time travel.
Scientists have debunked the idea that we’d

00:00:32.629 --> 00:00:36.559
ever be able to travel into the past. But
that hasn’t stopped physicists from theorizing

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:38.379
how it would work, logically, without creating
a paradox.

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:42.800
This is sort of the internet's go-to explainer
on how time travel works in fiction.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.589
My name is Harrison Densmore and I'm a graphic
designer and writer. I created this infographic

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:50.409
about 7 years ago.
You can generally break down time travel in

00:00:50.409 --> 00:00:54.489
movies into one of three different styles.
There’s the multiverse timeline, the dynamic

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:58.479
timeline, and the fixed timeline.
In a multiverse timeline, there are an infinite

00:00:58.479 --> 00:01:02.839
number of parallel universes. Any time travel
immediately creates a new timeline, and the

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:06.910
traveler can do just about anything — only
that timeline is affected.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:11.640
Kill your grandfather, and you’ll have just
created a new universe where you don’t exist.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:17.680
This is what you see with Terminator 2 and
3, Rick and Morty, Doctor Who, and the 2009

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.540
Star Trek.
Then there’s what he calls the dynamic timeline,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.780
where altered events in the past have a real
impact on the present.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:28.640
Go back and try to kill your grandfather here,
and you create a paradox, a logical contradiction.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.890
If you time-traveled to kill your grandfather,
you wouldn’t exist in the future to travel

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.380
back in time to kill your grandfather.
And you can see the idea of a dynamic timeline

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.500
presented in Back to the Future.
It doesn’t really make sense, because the

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.210
traveler is returning to a present day that
he wouldn’t actually exist in.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.730
Then there’s the fixed timeline.
In a fixed timeline, when a person travels

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.000
back in time, they can’t change the future
that they just left. This is the version you

00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.640
see in the Prisoner of Azkaban, or 12 Monkeys.
Everything that they do just confirms the

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.210
present that they left. Try to kill your grandfather
here, and you’ll inevitably fail. You can’t

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.729
change the past.
And it seems like this is how Game of Thrones

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.110
interprets the idea.
It all starts with this moment right here,

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.810
when Bran is watching a young version of his
late father:

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:21.450
“Father!”
Because Ned Stark turns around seemingly in

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:26.010
response to Bran’s voice, it seems like
Bran might be able to change the past.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.310
So when the Raven says this:
"The past is already written. The ink is dry."

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.420
It sounds like he’s not telling the truth.
Clearly if Bran was able to get his father

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.920
to react to his voice, then it seems like
he has the power to change the past, right?

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.370
But what happens to Hodor dispels this idea.
When Bran takes control of Hodor’s body

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.600
in the present while also observing him in
the past, he creates a psychic link between

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.700
past and present that destroys Hodor’s mind.
So, Bran created Hodor as we've always known

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.310
him on the show.
That could mean Bran can affect the past,

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.700
but only in ways that lead to outcomes we
already see in the present.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.450
Bran’s powers seem to operate in what's
called a “closed timelike curve” in mathematical

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.180
physics. What that means is that all time
travel is consistent with its starting point

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.180
— every change he creates is already true
in his present world. That’s different from

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.380
Back to the Future, where actions in the past
produce a new, different future.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.400
The Game of Thrones approach to the theory
follows something called the Novikov self-consistency

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.780
principle.
It’s a physics conjecture that assumes a

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.489
single timeline, stating that if there is
an event that would cause a paradox, then

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:32.760
the probability of that event is zero.
In 1990, the Russian physicist Igor Dmitriyevich

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.459
Novikov teamed up with researchers from Caltech
to write a paper in which they argued that

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:41.020
there are no unresolvable paradoxes in the
singular timeline framework. That means that

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.090
no matter what a theoretical time traveler
does, they could never succeed to create a

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.660
situation that doesn’t work logically — they
would never be able to succeed in killing

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.020
their grandfather, for example.
And that is basically what appears to be happening

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.319
in Game of Thrones.
It seems like Bran can interact with past,

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:00.680
but only in ways that confirm the existing
present reality. Unfortunately for Bran, that

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.410
means that he can’t change any of the bad
things that have happened to him.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.580
But it opens up the possibility that he could
be the source behind a lot of major events.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.940
The internet already has a lot of theories.
He could turn out to be the voices in the

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.930
Mad King’s head — or partly responsible
for the creation of the Wall itself.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:20.180
And that could make him the most important
character in the whole story — for all we

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.500
know, he could be orchestrating everything
we’ve seen in the show so far.

